<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nallur Kandaswamy Kovil</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nallur Kandaswamy Temple</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nallur">Nallur</a> Kandaswamy Kovil is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple dedicated to the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kartikeya">Murugan</a> at Nallur, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffna">Jaffna</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. The original temple, linked with the kings of Jaffna, was destroyed by the Portuguese in the 17th century; the present building was raised in the 18th century under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rule. Its annual festival is one of the largest gatherings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lankan-tamils">Sri Lankan Tamils</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nallur-kandaswamy-kovil.jpg" alt="Nallur Kandaswamy Kovil" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gane Kumaraswamy · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nallur_Kandasamy_front_entrance.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
